3.text:
The real 1-parameter family

\begin{equation*}
{\mathcal C}_{E}:\qquad y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}\;,
\qquad 
T(E)=\sqrt{2}\oint_{{\mathcal C}_{E}}\frac{dq}{y},
\end{equation*}
is the usual hyperelliptic family.  For $n=2k$ the involution
$q\mapsto -q$ makes the Jacobian split and the invariant part of the
Gauss–Manin system is of rank

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1 .
\end{equation*}
For $n=2k+1$ the invariant part is the whole system and

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, apart from the degenerate case $n=2$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\iff n\equiv 0\pmod 2 ,\qquad 
u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1\iff n\pm1\equiv1\pmod2,
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the trivial $n=2$ case, the first non–trivial
admissible exponent is

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and $n_{1}/2=2$ is the first exponent for which the invariant
part of the Gauss–Manin connection is of rank $n_{1}/2-1$ and the two
neighbouring odd families are the ordinary $u_{r}(m)=m-1$ families.
Putting

\begin{equation*}
m_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,\qquad 
c_{1}=\frac1{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}=\frac14,
\end{equation*}
the Hamiltonian which is usually written in the normal form

\begin{equation*}
H_{1}(p,q)=\frac12p^{2}+\frac12q^{2}-c_{1}q^{m_{1}}
           =\frac12p^{2}+\frac12\bigl(1-2c_{1}\bigr)q^{2},
\end{equation*}
is the ordinary harmonic oscillator

\begin{equation*}
\omega_{1}^{2}=1-2c_{1}=1-\frac12=\frac12 ,
\qquad 
H_{1}=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{\omega_{1}^{2}}2q^{2}+{\rm const} .
\end{equation*}
Hence for every admissible energy $E$ the real period is the ordinary
harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
T_{{\rm phys}}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{1}}
                 =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-2c_{1}}}=2\sqrt2\,\pi ,
\end{equation*}
in particular it is independent of the energy parameter
$\alpha$.  In the usual hypergeometric normalisation
$T_{0}(0)=\pi$, i.e. $T_{0}(E)=\pi\,{}_{2}F_{1}(\dots;\alpha)$,
the ${}_{2}F_{1}$–factor is identically $1$ for the quadratic case and

\begin{equation*}
T_{0}(\alpha)\equiv T_{0}(0)=\pi ,
\end{equation*}
so that, with the conventional $2\pi$-normalisation,
$T_{\rm phys}(\alpha)\equiv2\pi/\omega_{1}=2\sqrt2\,\pi$.  In
particular, with the customary $\pi$-normalisation,
$T_{0}\bigl((n_{1}-1)/n_{2}\bigr)=T_{0}(1/2)=T_{0}(0)$,
i.e. up to the inessential factor $2$ the (real) hypergeometric
period is the constant $\pi$; in physical normalisation this is
$T_{\rm phys}(1/2)=2\sqrt2\,\pi$, i.e. $\sqrt2\,\pi$ in the
$\pi$-normalisation.  The degenerate pair $(n_{1},n_{2})=(2,4)$ gives
the formal limit $\omega_{1}=1$, $T_{0}\equiv\pi$, $T_{\rm phys}\equiv
2\pi$, i.e. $T\bigl((n_{1}-1)/n_{2}\bigr)=T(1/4)=2\pi$, the usual
harmonic oscillator period.  This is the constant value quoted in the
literature. ∎

